<!-- livebook:{"persist_outputs":true} -->

# Example: a credit card application

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ecto_sql, "~> 3.13"},
    {:postgrex, "~> 0.22"},
    {:jason, "~> 1.4"},
    {:journey, "~> 0.11"},
    {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ],
  start_applications: false
)

Application.put_env(:journey, :log_level, :warning)

# Configure more frequent background sweeper runs (the default is 60 seconds).
# The precision of the timer is determined by the granularity of the sweeper.
Application.put_env(:journey, :background_sweeper, period_seconds: 5)

# This livebook requires a PostgreSQL database.
# If you don't have one running, you can start one with Docker:
# docker run --rm --name journey-livebook-postgres -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -d postgres:16

# Update this configuration to point to your database server
Application.put_env(:journey, Journey.Repo,
  database: "journey_credit_card_application",
  username: "postgres",
  password: "postgres",
  hostname: "localhost",
  log: false,
  port: 5432
)

Application.put_env(:journey, :ecto_repos, [Journey.Repo])

Journey.Repo.__adapter__().storage_up(Journey.Repo.config())

Application.loaded_applications()
|> Enum.map(fn {app, _, _} -> app end)
|> Enum.each(&Application.ensure_all_started/1)
```

## DB Setup

This livebook requires a PostgreSQL service. If you don't have one running, you can start one with Docker:

```bash
docker run --rm --name journey-livebook-postgres -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -d postgres:16
```

## What We'll Cover

The other livebooks each focus on a single node type. This one puts several of them together, walking through a credit card application from start to finish:

1. the customer provides their personal information, which kicks off a pre-approval process
2. the customer's SSN is erased once it is no longer needed
3. a pre-approved customer who hasn't requested a card gets a scheduled reminder
4. the customer requests a card, which initiates its issuance
5. the card gets mailed, the customer is notified, and the execution archives itself

Along the way, we'll see `input`, `compute`, `mutate`, `tick_once` and `archive` nodes, and conditional dependencies (`unblocked_when`).

## The Graph

The graph ships with Journey, as `Journey.Examples.CreditCardApplication.graph/0`. You can read its definition, and the "business logic" functions it calls, in [lib/journey/examples/credit_card_application.ex](https://github.com/shipworthy/journey/blob/main/lib/journey/examples/credit_card_application.ex).

```elixir
graph = Journey.Examples.CreditCardApplication.graph()

graph
|> Journey.Tools.generate_mermaid_graph()
|> Kino.Mermaid.new()
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```mermaid
graph TD
    %% Graph
    subgraph Graph["🧩 'Credit Card Application flow graph', version v1.0.0"]
        execution_id[execution_id]
        last_updated_at[last_updated_at]
        full_name[full_name]
        birth_date[birth_date]
        ssn[ssn]
        email_address[email_address]
        credit_score[["credit_score<br/>(fetch_credit_score)"]]
        ssn_redacted[["ssn_redacted<br/>(anonymous fn)<br/>mutates: ssn"]]
        preapproval_decision[["preapproval_decision<br/>(compute_decision)"]]
        congratulate[["congratulate<br/>(send_congrats)"]]
        inform_of_rejection[["inform_of_rejection<br/>(send_rejection)"]]
        preapproval_process_completed[["preapproval_process_completed<br/>(all_done)"]]
        schedule_request_credit_card_reminder[["schedule_request_credit_card_reminder<br/>(choose_the_time_to_send_reminder)<br/>:tick_once"]]
        credit_card_requested[credit_card_requested]
        send_preapproval_reminder[["send_preapproval_reminder<br/>(send_preapproval_reminder)"]]
        initiate_credit_card_issuance[["initiate_credit_card_issuance<br/>(request_credit_card_issuance)"]]
        credit_card_mailed[credit_card_mailed]
        credit_card_mailed_notification[["credit_card_mailed_notification<br/>(send_card_mailed_notification)"]]
        schedule_archival[["schedule_archival<br/>(choose_the_time_to_archive)<br/>:tick_once"]]
        archive[["archive<br/>:archive"]]

        full_name -->  credit_score
        birth_date -->  credit_score
        ssn -->  credit_score
        email_address -->  credit_score
        credit_score -->  ssn_redacted
        credit_score -->  preapproval_decision
        full_name -->  preapproval_decision
        preapproval_decision --> |approved?| congratulate
        preapproval_decision --> |rejected?| inform_of_rejection
        congratulate -->  preapproval_process_completed
        inform_of_rejection -->  preapproval_process_completed
        congratulate -->  schedule_request_credit_card_reminder
        schedule_request_credit_card_reminder -->  send_preapproval_reminder
        credit_card_requested --> |not true?| send_preapproval_reminder
        credit_card_requested --> |true?| initiate_credit_card_issuance
        credit_card_mailed --> |true?| credit_card_mailed_notification
        initiate_credit_card_issuance -->  credit_card_mailed_notification
        last_updated_at --> |long_time_since_last_update?| schedule_archival
        credit_card_mailed_notification -->  schedule_archival
        inform_of_rejection -->  schedule_archival
        schedule_archival -->  archive
    end

    %% Styling
    classDef defaultNode fill:#f8f9fa,stroke:#495057,stroke-width:2px,color:#000000

    %% Apply styles to nodes
    class execution_id,last_updated_at,full_name,birth_date,ssn,email_address,credit_score,ssn_redacted,preapproval_decision,congratulate,inform_of_rejection,preapproval_process_completed,schedule_request_credit_card_reminder,credit_card_requested,send_preapproval_reminder,initiate_credit_card_issuance,credit_card_mailed,credit_card_mailed_notification,schedule_archival,archive defaultNode
```

A few things to note:

* `:full_name`, `:birth_date`, `:ssn`, `:email_address`, `:credit_card_requested` and `:credit_card_mailed` are `input` nodes, set by the customer or by the fulfillment department.
* `:credit_score` and `:preapproval_decision` are `compute` nodes, computed as soon as their upstream dependencies are provided.
* `:congratulate` and `:inform_of_rejection` are conditional: only one of them gets unblocked, depending on the value of `:preapproval_decision`.
* `:ssn_redacted` is a `mutate` node: once `:credit_score` is computed, it overwrites `:ssn`.
* `:schedule_request_credit_card_reminder` and `:schedule_archival` are `tick_once` nodes, scheduling `:send_preapproval_reminder` and `:archive`.

## The Customer Applies

The customer starts the application process and provides their personal information:

```elixir
execution =
  graph
  |> Journey.start()
  |> Journey.set(:full_name, "Mario")
  |> Journey.set(:birth_date, "10/11/1981")
  |> Journey.set(:ssn, "123-45-6789")
  |> Journey.set(:email_address, "mario@example.com");
:ok
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Pre-approval

Providing all four inputs unblocks `:credit_score`, which in turn unblocks `:preapproval_decision`, and so on. Each of the simulated "business logic" functions takes about a second, so this takes a few seconds:

```elixir
{:ok, true, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :preapproval_process_completed, wait: :any)
{:ok, credit_score, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :credit_score)
{:ok, decision, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :preapproval_decision)
"credit score: #{credit_score}, decision: #{decision}"
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"credit score: 800, decision: approved"
```

The customer was approved, so `:congratulate` ran, and `:inform_of_rejection` didn't:

```elixir
{Journey.get(execution, :congratulate), Journey.get(execution, :inform_of_rejection)}
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{{:ok, "email_sent_congrats", 12}, {:error, :not_set}}
```

## The SSN Is Gone

Once the credit score has been computed, we no longer need the customer's SSN. The `:ssn_redacted` mutate node has replaced it:

```elixir
Journey.get(execution, :ssn)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:ok, "<redacted>", 3}
```

## A Reminder

Once the customer was congratulated, `:schedule_request_credit_card_reminder` scheduled a reminder, a few seconds out (in a real application, this would be a few days). When the time arrives, and since the customer still hasn't requested a card, `:send_preapproval_reminder` runs:

```elixir
{:ok, true, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :send_preapproval_reminder, wait: :any)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:ok, true, 18}
```

## The Customer Requests a Card

Reminded, the customer requests a credit card, which initiates its issuance:

```elixir
execution = Journey.set(execution, :credit_card_requested, true)
{:ok, true, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :initiate_credit_card_issuance, wait: :any)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:ok, true, 22}
```

Here is where things stand, with the remaining nodes waiting for the card to be mailed:

```elixir
execution.id
|> Journey.Tools.generate_mermaid_execution()
|> Kino.Mermaid.new()
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

