## The Card Is Mailed

Eventually, the fulfillment department marks the card as mailed, which triggers a notification to the customer:

```elixir
execution = Journey.set(execution, :credit_card_mailed, true)
{:ok, true, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :credit_card_mailed_notification, wait: :any)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:ok, true, 25}
```

## The Execution Archives Itself

With the card in the mail, there is nothing left to do. `:schedule_archival` scheduled the archival a few seconds out, and when the time arrives, the `:archive` node archives the execution:

```elixir
{:ok, archived_at, _revision} = Journey.get(execution, :archive, wait: :any)
archived_at |> DateTime.from_unix!() |> Calendar.strftime("%H:%M:%S UTC")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"00:35:42 UTC"
```

The archived execution is no longer returned by `Journey.load/2`:

```elixir
Journey.load(execution.id)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
nil
```

It is still there if you need it, though. For example, we can introspect it:

```elixir
Journey.Tools.introspect(execution.id) |> IO.puts()
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

